# Technical Proposal: Real-Time Output Guardrails & Cognitive Firewall

## **The Big Picture: Securing the Final Exit Point**

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[AI Agent Output] --> B{PII-Safe Guardrail}
    B -->|Search via Trie| C[Mapping Check]
    B -->|Search via Trie| D[Honey-Trap Check]
    C -->|Found Leak| E[Redact with Pseudonym]
    D -->|Found Trap| F[Flag Breach & Redact]
    E --> G[Safe Sanitized Output]
    F --> G
```

---

## **Layered Output Defense: How it works**

### **Layer 1: Outcome Correction (PII Mapping)**
We perform a final "Sweep" of the AI's output for known PII. If the LLM output contains a raw value (e.g., `ali@email.com`), it is automatically swapped back to its pseudonym (`[USER_1]`).

### **Layer 2: Behavioral Detection (Granular Honey-Tokens)**
We inject unique, high-entropy "Trap Tokens" (Canaries). The presence of any trap in the output is a **Deterministic Indicator of Compromise (IoC)**.

---

## **The Algorithmic Flow (Trie Matching Mechanics)**

```mermaid
graph LR
    Root((Root)) --> P((P))
    P --> I1((I))
    I1 --> I2((I))
    I2 -->|Match Found| Out1[PII Found!]
    I2 -->|Fail Link| S((_))
    S --> A((A))
    A --> F((F))
    F --> E((E))
    E -->|Match Found| Out2[PII_SAFE Found!]
```

### **Fail-Link: The Smart Search Continuity**

One common question in multi-pattern matching is: *"If a specific word match fails, why do we continue scanning?"*

Our engine doesn't just search for "words"; it monitors the **entire text stream**. Here is why the "Fail-Link" is essential:

- **The Radar Analogy:** Even if we were looking for `test@gmail.com` and the AI outputs `tes@gmail.com`, we don't stop. The `@` sign that caused the "failure" for the first email might be the exact middle point for another forbidden email (e.g., `admin@gmail.com`).
- **Zero-Waste Scanning:** The Fail-Link ensures that we never skip a character. If a match fails at node X, we jump to the next best node in the Trie that matches our current suffix. This ensures **Total Coverage** without having to re-read any character.

---

## **Performance: Complexity & Scalability**

We utilize the **Aho-Corasick Automaton**, which ensures that the scanning speed is independent of the number of items we are guarding.

### **Efficiency Analysis Table**

| Metric | Traditional Search | **Our Aho-Corasick Engine** |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Search Complexity** | **O(Text Length * Secret Count)** | **O(Text Length only)** |
| **Passes over Output** | Multiple Passes | **Single-Pass (Atomic)** |
| **Scalability** | Slows down as secrets increase | **Maintains speed as secrets increase** |

---

## **Engineering Implementation Snippets**

### **1. Building the Search Tree (From Map Contents)**
We take the sensitive keys from our Session Map and build a character-by-character search tree.

```python
def add_pattern(self, pattern):
    node = 0
    # We walk through each character and create a branch if it's new.
    # This is how we map out the 'forbidden paths' in one tree.
    for char in pattern:
        if char not in self.trie[node]['next']:
            self.trie.append({'next': {}, 'fail': 0, 'output': []})
            self.trie[node]['next'][char] = len(self.trie) - 1
        node = self.trie[node]['next'][char]
    
    # Once we hit the last character, we mark this path as a full match.
    self.trie[node]['output'].append(pattern)
```

### **2. The Security Filter (Unified Guardrail)**
This is where the magic happens. We scan for both real PII and our secret traps in one single pass for maximum speed.

```python
def run_output_guardrail(text, token_map, honey_tokens):
    # We combine everything we want to catch into one search list.
    # Traps get a special mask, and real PII gets its specific pseudonym.
    unified_map = {trap: "[REDACTED_SECURITY_SENSITIVE]" for trap in honey_tokens}
    unified_map.update(token_map)
    
    # Fire up the high-speed search engine
    ac_engine = AhoCorasick(list(unified_map.keys()))
    clean_text, count, found_patterns = ac_engine.search_and_replace(text, unified_map)
    
    # Security Forensics: Did we find any of our secret honey-tokens?
    matched_traps = [p for p in found_patterns if p in honey_tokens]
    if matched_traps:
        # If a trap is triggered, we know an exfiltration attempt occurred.
        is_compromised = True 
```

